Post-installation configuration
===============================

You will want to consider doing the following after installing this
package:

* Install mysql-server, for a local database.

* Alternatively, set up a connection to a remote MySQL server:
  - rm /etc/mysql/koha-common.cnf
  - $EDITOR /etc/mysql/koha-common.cnf

        [client]
        host = some.other.host
        user = mysqlusername
        password = mysqlpassword
        
* Enable the rewrite module: a2enmod rewrite

* Listen on port 8080: $EDITOR /etc/apache2/ports.conf

* Alternatively, configure koha-create, using /etc/koha/koha-sites.conf
  (see /usr/sbin/koha-create for which variables to set).
  
* After that, you can create a new Koha instance:
  - koha-create --create-db name


Background job notifications
============================

New installations use JobsNotificationMethod=polling. Background workers
check the database for new jobs every ten seconds and do not require a
message broker. The workers must continue to run in either mode.

Existing settings are preserved on upgrade, including STOMP. Koha's
maintainer scripts do not remove or stop RabbitMQ. Koha package upgrades
configure and restart a local broker only if an instance uses STOMP with a
local broker hostname; remote brokers are managed on their own host.

If you keep a local broker that was installed automatically as a dependency,
protect it from apt autoremove by marking it manually installed:

  apt-mark manual rabbitmq-server

To use a local RabbitMQ broker on a new installation:

* Install rabbitmq-server explicitly.
* Enable its STOMP plugin: rabbitmq-plugins enable --offline rabbitmq_stomp
* Restart the broker: service rabbitmq-server restart
* Configure the message_broker section in the instance's koha-conf.xml.
* Select STOMP in JobsNotificationMethod and restart the background workers.

A remote broker only needs its connection settings in koha-conf.xml and
STOMP selected in JobsNotificationMethod; no local rabbitmq-server package
is required.

To switch an existing instance to polling, select polling and restart both
worker queues, replacing INSTANCE with its Koha instance name:

  koha-worker --restart --queue default INSTANCE
  koha-worker --restart --queue long_tasks INSTANCE

Check all Koha instances and other broker users before stopping or removing
a broker that is no longer needed by this instance.


Koha filesystem layout on Debian
================================

The Debian packaging of Koha puts files in the following places:

* /etc/koha -- system configuration files
* /etc/cron.hourly/koha-common -- cron job
* /etc/cron.daily/koha-common -- cron job
* /etc/cron.d/koha-common -- cron job
* /usr/share/koha -- shared files (HTML templates, Perl code, etc)

Each Koha instance has files in the following places:

* /etc/koha/sites/$name -- confguration files
* /etc/apache2/sites-available/$name -- Apache config file
* /var/lib/koha/$name -- Zebra databases
* /var/log/koha/$name -- log files (Apache, Zebra)
* /var/lock/koha/$name -- run-time lock files
* /var/run/koha/$name -- run-time sockets etc
* /var/spool/koha/$name -- database dumps


Koha and MySQL
==============

koha-common's postinst script sets up the /etc/mysql/koha-common.cnf,
and all scripts use that to access the MySQL database. By default, it
is a symlink pointing at debian.cnf, and only works on localhost.
If you wish, you can create a file /etc/mysql/koha-common.cnf instead
of the symlink, and point it at a remote server. There is currently
no tool to help you do that, but it should be fairly straightforward.
